Yes. A CAP/DA beneficiary can transfer, and the governing rule is NC Medicaid Clinical Coverage Policy 3K-2, section 3.3, which requires the Case Management Entity (CME) to coordinate the transfer so there are no gaps in services. The policy was effective September 1, 2025, and the amendments check returned 3K-2 as the current authoritative document. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Transfer rules for eligible CAP/DA beneficiaries

Under section 3.3, before a transfer occurs, the CME must:

- determine the anticipated start date of services;
- coordinate a transition plan between provider agencies;
- discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- initiate transfer of the electronic health record in the CAP Business System (e-CAP/DA) to the receiving county;
- arrange a home visit within 5 business days after the beneficiary moves to the new primary residence, so the receiving entity can assess the environment and identify health and welfare concerns; and
- coordinate services so they begin on the first date of the transfer. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## If the beneficiary is transferring to a different county

Section 3.3 adds specific county-transfer requirements:

- the transferring CME designee must coordinate with the receiving CME designee on the agreed transfer date, or within less than 30 calendar days of the request to transfer;
- both agencies must discuss and plan for the beneficiary’s health, safety, and well-being;
- the electronic health record must be transferred to the receiving county;
- the receiving CME designee must arrange the home visit within 5 business days of the move; and
- services must be coordinated to start on the first day of the transfer into the receiving county. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## Beneficiary choice of provider

The policy also supports provider changes through its person-centered planning rules. The CAP/DA service plan must document the authorized provider, and it must ensure the beneficiary’s right to choose among providers and among Medicaid-enrolled providers, as shown by a signed freedom-of-choice form. That is strong policy support that beneficiaries may change providers, provided the transfer is coordinated through the CAP/DA process. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

## What I could not verify

I could verify clear rules for transferring an eligible CAP/DA beneficiary, especially for county transfers and continuity of service. I could not verify from the retrieved text any separate, more detailed subsection laying out a standalone “provider-to-provider transfer” procedure beyond the coordination steps in section 3.3 and the beneficiary’s freedom of choice in the service plan requirements.

## Federal authority

I did not find transfer rules for CAP/DA beneficiaries in the retrieved federal section, 42 CFR 413.64. That regulation concerns payment rules to providers, not beneficiary transfer procedures. The amendments check returned 42 CFR Part 413 as current, but it does not appear to govern the transfer question here. [42 CFR Part 413](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-413)

