**Federal authority:** NC’s CAP/DA waiver is a **home- and community-based services (HCBS) waiver under § 1915(c) of the Social Security Act**, as implemented through **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**. The federal regulation repeatedly frames these as waiver requests under “this subpart,” and specifically refers to the State’s **1915(c) HCBS waiver** requirements. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441). I could verify the statute document contains **Section 1915**, but the retrieved text here did not surface subsection **1915(c)** itself, so the clearest text I can quote from the corpus is the implementing federal regulation rather than the statute’s subsection text. The CAP/DA clinical policy is current in the corpus with effective date **Sept. 1, 2025** and no amendment returned by the amendment check; 42 CFR Part 441 also returned as current. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment); [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## What the state must show in its waiver application

Under **42 CFR § 441.301**, a waiver request must include:

- the **assurances required by § 441.302** and the **supporting documentation required by § 441.303**;
- any requested waivers of Medicaid requirements such as **statewideness, comparability, or certain medically needy income/resource rules**, when applicable;
- whether the state will refuse HCBS when it reasonably expects the cost to exceed institutional care costs; and
- if HCBS are furnished under the waiver, the request must provide that services are:
  - delivered under a **written person-centered service plan** approved by the Medicaid agency;
  - furnished only to people who are **not inpatients** of a hospital, nursing facility, or ICF/IID; and
  - furnished only to people the state determines **would otherwise require** the Medicaid-covered level of care provided in a hospital, nursing facility, or ICF/IID. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

The application also must:

- describe who is qualified to develop the plan of care;
- describe the **target group(s)** to be served;
- describe the **services** separately and clearly; and
- include required **person-centered planning** features and person-centered service plan requirements. For example, the planning process must be led by the individual (with representative if applicable), include informed choice, address conflicts of interest, and record alternative HCBS settings considered. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

Under **42 CFR § 441.303**, the state must give CMS enough documentation to support those assurances, including:

- **health and welfare safeguards** for beneficiaries;
- records and information supporting **financial accountability**;
- the state’s plan for **evaluation and reevaluation** of beneficiaries, including:
  - who performs evaluations,
  - how they are performed,
  - the evaluation form used,
  - written documentation procedures, and
  - regular reevaluation procedures;
- how beneficiaries will be informed of feasible alternatives and allowed to choose **institutional care or HCBS**;
- how the state will apply post-eligibility income/resource rules where applicable; and
- documentation supporting **cost neutrality**—that average per-capita waiver costs will not exceed the average per-capita cost of services absent the waiver. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

Section 441.303 also requires the state to state the number of **unduplicated beneficiaries** it plans to serve each year, which acts as a waiver cap unless CMS approves an amendment. For people diverted from institutions rather than discharged from them, the evaluation process must be detailed enough to ensure waiver services are limited to people who truly would otherwise need the relevant institutional level of care. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## NC implementation context for CAP/DA

NC’s CAP/DA policy reflects those federal waiver concepts. The policy describes CAP/DA as an HCBS waiver program with three participation options: **provider-led, consumer-led, and coordinated caregiving**. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

The NC policy also lists state assurances and performance areas such as:

- participant access,
- participant-centered service planning,
- provider capacity,
- participant safeguards,
- participant rights and responsibilities,
- participant outcomes and satisfaction, and
- system performance. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment)

That NC language is **implementation context**, not the core federal rule. The governing federal application requirements remain **§ 441.301** and **§ 441.303**. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

## Bottom line

For NC’s CAP/DA waiver, the federal statutory basis is **Social Security Act § 1915(c)**, and the implementing federal rules are in **42 CFR Part 441, Subpart G**. In its waiver application, NC must show at least:

- the waiver’s **legal/requested exceptions** from ordinary Medicaid rules, if any;
- the **target group** to be served;
- the **institutional level of care** participants would otherwise require;
- the **HCBS services** to be offered;
- a compliant **person-centered planning and service plan** process;
- **health and welfare safeguards**;
- **financial accountability**;
- a valid **evaluation/reevaluation** system;
- **freedom of choice** between HCBS and institutional care; and
- **cost neutrality** and projected participant numbers. [42 CFR Part 441](https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-42/part-441)

