Under CAP/DA, home modifications must be tied to the beneficiary’s assessed needs and approved plan of care, and they must be aimed at improving the beneficiary’s mobility, safety, independence, community integration, environmental/community accessibility, or addressing 24/7 coverage concerns. The beneficiary also agrees to work with the multidisciplinary team to identify needs for equipment, modification, and technology. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment) (effective Sept. 1, 2025), § 3.4.5; “I agree to:”.

## What counts as a home modification
The policy’s equipment/modification/technology section includes examples such as:

- ramps, grab bars, safety rails, and handrails;
- widening doorways or passages for wheelchair or walker access;
- medically necessary emergency egress;
- bathroom modifications for accessibility;
- bedroom modifications to accommodate hospital beds and wheelchairs;
- kitchen modifications for accessibility;
- floor coverings for ease of ambulation;
- certain lifts and transfer devices used in the home; and
- replacement of fixtures, mirrors, and storage space damaged or removed during an approved modification.

The policy limits these modifications to qualifying residences, including:

- the beneficiary’s primary private residence if owned by the beneficiary or family;
- a rented residence if the modifications are portable; or
- a rented residence if the owner allows the modification. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “EQUIPMENT, MODIFICAITON AND TECHNOLOGY” (effective Sept. 1, 2025).

## Required documentation from the Multidisciplinary Team
For a requested home modification, the policy says the MDT documentation must include:

- a recommendation by the MDT identifying the beneficiary’s needs regarding the requested home modification;
- the cost of the item or modification required by the beneficiary;
- a statement of need identified in the service plan;
- evidence of medical need recommended by the MDT or by a physician, when applicable;
- shipping costs itemized in the proposal request;
- any other information required for the specific modification requested;
- required quotes for purchase, adaptation, or modification, in the number NC Medicaid requires; and
- written recommendations from whatever professional(s) NC Medicaid or its designee determines are appropriate when special circumstances exist. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.9.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025).

## Additional documentation the case manager/MDT request must contain
The policy also requires documentation in the request file showing:

- a comprehensive multidisciplinary declaration-of-need assessment completed by the case manager identifying the equipment, technology, supply, adaptation, or modification needs;
- a copy of the physician’s order, when applicable;
- a recommendation by an appropriate professional identifying the beneficiary’s need for the requested adaptation or modification;
- the estimated life of the item/modification and the length of time the beneficiary is expected to benefit from it; and
- an invoice from the supplier showing the date provided and the cost, including related charges, maintained in the CAP Business system. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), “Limits, Amount And Frequency” (effective Sept. 1, 2025).

## Training documentation
If the request involves equipment or technology associated with the modification, the policy also requires:

- a plan for training the beneficiary and family on use of the equipment; and
- documentation by the case manager that the training was completed, signed by the CAP/DA beneficiary or responsible party. [Community Alternatives Program for Disabled Adults (CAP/DA)](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/3k-2-community-alternatives-program-disabled-adults-capda/download?attachment), § 5.9.1 (effective Sept. 1, 2025).

