NC Medicaid does **not** cover certain ambulance services and charges under **Clinical Coverage Policy 15, Ambulance Services**, effective **Dec. 15, 2023**. The policy’s item-specific non-covered list includes: ([Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment))

- when an ambulance is called and **no treatment is needed**;
- when the ambulance responds to a **false alarm**;
- when the **beneficiary refuses all medical services**;
- ambulance transport for a **medical service that is not a Medicaid-covered service**;
- **commercial airline tickets**;
- **airstrip fees**;
- **taxes** charged separately (local, state, federal, etc.);
- **separate additional charges for nursing personnel** who are employees of a facility or ambulance service;
- **waiting fees**; and
- **separate charges for oxygen, items, and supplies**, because those are included in the base rate and are not separately reimbursable.  
  *Policy 15 § 4.2.1.4; § 4.2.1.* ([Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment))

The policy also says that **services other than those listed as covered in Section 3.2 are not covered**. *Policy 15 § 4.2.1.4; see also § 3.2.* ([Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment))

More generally, ambulance services are also not covered if: ([Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment))

- the beneficiary does **not meet eligibility requirements** in Section 2.0;
- the beneficiary does **not meet the coverage criteria** in Section 3.0;
- the service **duplicates another provider’s service**; or
- the service is **experimental, investigational, or part of a clinical trial**.  
  *Policy 15 § 4.1.* ([Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment))

There is also a mileage limitation: if a beneficiary is transferred to a facility that is **not the nearest appropriate facility**, the **loaded mileage to that farther facility is not reimbursed**. Beneficiary preference or a physician’s staff privileges do not make another facility “appropriate.” *Policy 15 § 4.2.1.1.* ([Ambulance Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/15-ambulance-services/download?attachment))

I also checked for amendments, and the knowledge base identifies **Policy 15** as the current authoritative document, with **no separate amending change notice returned**.

Physicians should provide fluoride varnish **from tooth eruption until the beneficiary is 3½ years old**. NC Medicaid also limits billing for the varnish code to **beneficiaries under 3½ years of age**, **once every 60 calendar days**, and **no more than six times before the child reaches 3½**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

## Oral screening requirements

NC Medicaid’s physician fluoride varnish policy requires the oral screening package to include:

- **Early caries screening** and detection of notable findings in the mouth, including obvious hard- and soft-tissue pathology, using **a dental mirror and directed light**;
- **Counseling and educational materials** on good oral hygiene and nutrition for children;
- **Prescribing a fluoride supplement, if indicated**, following American Academy of Pediatrics guidelines;
- **Application of fluoride varnish** to all erupted primary teeth from tooth eruption until age 3½. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

## Documentation required in the health record

The beneficiary’s record must document all of the following:

- **An oral evaluation and any notable findings**;
- **Preventive oral health and dietary counseling** with the primary caregiver;
- **Application of fluoride varnish**; and
- **Referral to a dentist, if appropriate**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

## Current policy status

I checked amendments for this document, and the authoritative current document returned was **Clinical Coverage Policy 1A-23, Physician Fluoride Varnish Services**, effective **December 4, 2019**. [[Physician Fluoride Varnish Services](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/1a-23-physician-fluoride-varnish-services/download?attachment)]

