Under NC Medicaid, an HIV case management provider must meet both **initial certification** standards and **ongoing recertification/quality oversight** requirements. A provider can be **decertified** for a wide range of program, staffing, billing, documentation, confidentiality, and compliance failures.

## Certification requirements

NC Medicaid’s HIV Case Management policy requires the provider to:

- have a **documented successful record of three years** providing or managing HIV case management programs;
- ensure HIV case management services are provided by **qualified case managers** under the policy’s staff qualification rules;
- ensure HIV case managers are supervised by **qualified supervisors**;
- **enroll each physical site** with NC Medicaid as an HIV Case Management provider;
- meet all applicable **state and federal Medicaid participation requirements**;
- **maintain certification** as a qualified HIV Case Management provider;
- show compliance with **initial and ongoing certification processes**;
- show compliance with **monitoring and evaluation of case management records through a quality improvement plan**;
- allow NC Medicaid or its designated contractor to **review beneficiary, operational, and financial records**; and
- notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of proposed business changes within **30 calendar days of the proposed change** and no later than **5 business days after the actual change**.  
In addition, **within one year of Medicaid enrollment**, the provider must achieve **national accreditation** from one of the designated accrediting bodies listed in the policy. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.1 (effective 2020-01-15; amendment check returned the policy as current).

### Administrative/application-type requirements reflected in the policy
The policy also requires materials and operational readiness consistent with certification, including:

- a list of counties to be served;
- regular operating hours and an **emergency after-hours response plan**;
- a physical business site verified by **site visit** and not located in a private residence or vehicle;
- organizational and ownership information;
- access to required web-based systems;
- a business plan and financial information;
- computer capability for Medicaid IT, HIPAA, electronic records, analysis, billing, EFT, and communication requirements;
- completion of a **precertification onsite visit**;
- compliance with all applicable licensure/certification requirements; and
- written organizational policies such as confidentiality, grievance, beneficiary rights, nondiscrimination, ethics, conflict of interest, records, transfer/discharge, and abuse/neglect/exploitation policies. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), administrative requirements under § 6.2 (effective 2020-01-15; amendment check returned the policy as current).

### Recertification requirements
For recertification, the provider must:

- submit a **complete and signed Recertification Application** within **60 calendar days of receipt**;
- submit updated copies of items that changed since initial certification;
- submit copies of all HIV case manager and supervisor credentials;
- submit an annual summary of **quality improvement activities**, including outcome metrics; and
- undergo a **recertification on-site visit**, including review of records and other clinical/business documentation as needed. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.4 (effective 2020-01-15; amendment check returned the policy as current).

### Recertification checklist items
The HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist specifically asks for confirmation that the submission includes, among other things:

- only one physical location per application;
- a title page and signed checklist;
- lists of all case managers and supervisors with employment dates;
- counties where HIV case management services are provided;
- services offered by the agency;
- operating hours and emergency after-hours plan;
- accrediting agency name and dates, or an explanation;
- satisfaction survey frequency and use of results; and
- counts of Medicaid and Ryan White clients. [dma-3157 HIV Case Management Provider Recertification Application Checklist](https://policies.ncdhhs.gov/wp-content/uploads/dma-3157.pdf) (amendment check returned the checklist as current).

## When a provider can be decertified

NC Medicaid may decertify an HIV case management provider for any of the following reasons:

- failure to provide **core service components**;
- **fraudulent billing practices**;
- owner conviction of a **felony charge**;
- failure to develop, submit, and implement a **written plan of correction** within **30 calendar days** for unmet program requirements cited by NC Medicaid or its designated contractor;
- **falsification of records**;
- violation of a beneficiary’s **confidentiality**;
- employing staff who do not meet policy qualification criteria;
- failure of staff to complete required **basic training within 90 days of employment**;
- failure of staff to obtain required **continuing education units (CEUs)**;
- failure to provide staff supervision sufficient to meet beneficiaries’ needs;
- failure to submit required documentation within policy or agency timeframes;
- failure to maintain documentation sufficient to support billing, including documentation required by § 7.5;
- failure to implement and enforce a **quality improvement program**;
- failure to timely notify NC Medicaid or its designated contractor of agency name, director, ownership, mailing address, or phone changes, where that results in inability to contact the provider;
- failure to comply with applicable **federal and state laws, regulations, reimbursement plan, and Medicaid policies**;
- failure to enroll any beneficiaries within **four months of certification**; or
- failure to achieve or maintain the certification requirements in § 6.2. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 6.2.5 (effective 2020-01-15; amendment check returned the policy as current).

## Oversight relevant to decertification
NC Medicaid or its designated contractor conducts **quality assurance site visits** that include:

- medical record reviews and comparison of Medicaid billing records to provider documentation;
- review of staff qualifications and training;
- chart review of case management services and adherence to core service/documentation requirements;
- investigation of complaints; and
- review of quality improvement activity compliance. [Human Immunodeficiency Virus (HIV) Case Management](https://medicaid.ncdhhs.gov/12b-human-immunodeficiency-virus-hiv-case-management/download?attachment), § 7.8 (effective 2020-01-15; amendment check returned the policy as current).

## Bottom line
To stay certified, an HIV case management provider must maintain qualified staff and supervision, properly enroll each site, obtain accreditation within one year, comply with documentation and quality-improvement rules, cooperate with reviews, and timely complete recertification. Decertification can follow not just fraud or criminal issues, but also staffing, training, documentation, supervision, quality-improvement, notification, and general compliance failures.

